  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 359 PM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nBy Monday and the start of the long term, mid-level ridging situated \nacross the midsection of the country will begin migrating eastward. \nChances for showers and thunderstorms will largely be focused along \nthe front (which may slow down or speed up as we approach the short \nterm), the best of which (60-80%) appear to be across the far \nsoutheastern portion of the forecast area. Sensible effects of the \nfront will be fairly substantial given the preceding heat, with \nhighs as much as 8-10 degrees \"cooler\" (relatively speaking) than \nSunday -- in the lower 80s to lower 90s. Portions of northeast \nGeorgia at elevation may even remain in the 70s. Expect further fine \ntuning of Monday's temperatures as they're directly tied to frontal \nprogress. \n\nTuesday and beyond, the core of the aforementioned mid-level ridging \nwill move overhead, accompanied by high pressure at the surface --\noriented such that we're likely to have an extended period of \"cold\" \nair damming. Heat will begin to build once again, with highs \nreturning to several degrees above average by mid-to-late week. \nBreezy easterly winds should support ample mixing during the \nafternoon, which will initially serve to keep heat index values \nwithin a few degrees of air temperatures. Widespread convective \ndevelopment will be hampered by subsidence, with rain chances \nrelegated largely to portions of south central Georgia through \nWednesday. \n\nThe presiding mid-level ridge will retrograde westward across the \nFour Corners region on Thursday, allowing for more substantial \nmoisture intrusion across the Southeast, and a return to more \nuniform afternoon thunderstorm chances areawide.",
